{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1571 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f2723e4bc9552192f65b11adae61940612ff78a2a8dd340d86b1ed55204342ad" :edb/db-before-t 1570 :edb/db-after-t 1571 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534883 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:03:28.968Z" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119570 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186119570 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119570 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= ⟨9,-2, 3⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z - 56 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 false] [17592186119570 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119571 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186119571 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119571 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFrom the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle$. So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= ⟨ - 1, 4, 8⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z - 33 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 false] [17592186119571 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119572 1100 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi$, find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186119572 1100 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119572 1145 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y + z + 13 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -2x + y - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-13$. So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi$. Note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n(r - ⟨0, 0,-13⟩) \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 1⟩ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 false] [17592186119572 1145 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119573 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186119573 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119573 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v_{1} \\cdot v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-16i - 4j - 14k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z - 124 &= 0 \\\\\n8x + 2y + 7z + 62 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 false] [17592186119573 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186119756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317144 13194139534883 true] [17592186119756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317151 13194139534883 true] [17592186119756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317158 13194139534883 true] [17592186119756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317186 13194139534883 true] [17592186119756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317193 13194139534883 true] [17592186119757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317165 13194139534883 true] [17592186119757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317200 13194139534883 true] [17592186119757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317231 13194139534883 true] [17592186119757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317238 13194139534883 true] [17592186119757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317252 13194139534883 true] [17592186119758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317172 13194139534883 true] [17592186119758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317207 13194139534883 true] [17592186119758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317214 13194139534883 true] [17592186119758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317259 13194139534883 true] [17592186119759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317179 13194139534883 true] [17592186119759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317221 13194139534883 true] [17592186119759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317224 13194139534883 true] [17592186119759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317245 13194139534883 true] [17592186120216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534883 true] [17592186120216 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1, 0, 3)$ and is parallel to the vectors $v_{1} = ⟨5, 2, 1⟩$ and $v_{2} = ⟨0, 3, 3⟩$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186120216 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨5,2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,3,3⟩.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186120216 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186120217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534883 true] [17592186120217 1100 "Given the Cartesian equation $x - y + 4z + 16 = 0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r - p) \\cdot n = 0$." 13194139534883 false] [17592186120217 1100 "Given the Cartesian equation $x-y+4z+16=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186120217 1145 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-1,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z+16 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317145 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1089 "q-127964" 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i-j+2k.$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1113 #uuid "7afe98b9-885a-5ca1-b17c-6d52dba0b047" 13194139534883 true] [17592186317144 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,2,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =⟨5,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z-11 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317146 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317147 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317148 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317149 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317150 13194139534883 true] [17592186317145 1040 #edb/ref 17